    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warn",
      "title": "deber vs deber de",
      "text": "<b>Debe estudiar</b> = he must study (duty). <b>Debe de estar estudiando</b> = he is probably studying (guess). In everyday Spanish many speakers use <i>deber</i> for both, but written Spanish and exams keep the distinction. <b>Hay que</b> has no subject; there is no \"hay que yo\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "pagebreak"
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. With a gerund"
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.4,
        4.4,
        4.2,
        4.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Periphrasis",
        "Meaning",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "estar + gerund",
          "in progress",
          "Estoy leyendo.",
          "I am reading."
        ],
        [
          "seguir + gerund",
          "continue",
          "Sigo trabajando.",
          "I am still working."
        ],
        [
          "continuar + gerund",
          "continue",
          "Continúa lloviendo.",
          "It keeps raining."
        ],
        [
          "llevar + time + gerund",
          "for how long",
          "Llevo dos años estudiando.",
          "I have been studying for two years."
        ],
        [
          "ir + gerund",
          "gradual progress",
          "Voy mejorando.",
          "I am getting better."
        ],
        [
          "andar + gerund",
          "going around doing",
          "Anda buscando trabajo.",
          "He is looking for work."
        ],
        [
          "venir + gerund",
          "has been doing",
          "Vengo diciéndolo hace años.",
          "I have been saying it for years."
        ],
        [
          "acabar + gerund",
          "end up doing",
          "Acabó vendiendo la casa.",
          "He ended up selling the house."
        ],
        [
          "quedarse + gerund",
          "remain doing",
          "Se quedó mirando.",
          "He stayed looking."
        ],
        [
          "pasarse + time + gerund",
          "spend time doing",
          "Se pasó la tarde leyendo.",
          "He spent the afternoon reading."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "bullets",
      "items": [
        "<b>Seguir sin + infinitive</b> (still not): <i>Sigo sin entenderlo.</i> (I still do not understand it.)",
        "<b>Llevar sin + infinitive</b> (not for a period): <i>Llevo dos días sin dormir.</i> (I have not slept for two days.)",
        "<b>No dejar de + infinitive</b> (keep doing): <i>No deja de llover.</i> (It keeps on raining.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Llevo dos años estudiando",
      "text": "Dutch uses <i>ik studeer al twee jaar</i> (present) or <i>ik ben al twee jaar aan het studeren</i>. Spanish has this specific structure: <b>llevar + time + gerund</b>. <i>Estudio desde hace dos años</i> is also correct (see B1 PDFs)."
    },
